Epictetus: judgment, agency, and what depends on us (@greek-epictetus: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-schools-life, #source @greek-source-epictetus, #philosophy @greek-sceptics, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-sceptics) { r_35EWPVCFYNWZ0DYG5R5HM05TC2
**Read:** [A Selection from the Discourses of Epictetus with the Encheiridion, translated by George Long](../sources/Epictetus-Long.lingua#r_5VCYFDR3KPWTJ3T35X5MW4T4BD). Begin with the Manual, sections 1, 5, 17, 20, and 30. Continue through the complete short Manual, then use the selected Discourses to examine the reasoning behind its compressed advice.

**Portuguese alternative:** [O Encheirídion de Epicteto, Dinucci and Julien](../sources/Epictetus-Dinucci-Julien.lingua#r_0T9SP89H4SNVSN6XV25QY7EF9W). Read the same numbered Manual sections and compare the rendering of judgment, desire, agency, and duties.

**Preparation:** [the distinction between a claim and the reason for accepting it](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN). [Crito](../socratic/Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH) offers a comparison about action under constraint; [Aristotle](../aristotle/Practical-wisdom-and-friendship.lingua#r_6RZXS4EJH55PRPW61MB33DTCNM) offers one about practical judgment.

Ask what belongs to one's own agency and what depends on bodies, other people, institutions, or circumstances. Reconstruct the connection between this distinction and the treatment of impressions, desires, and distress. The force of the recommendation depends on the account of value; identify that account rather than treating the advice as a neutral description of control.

**Practice:** describe an ordinary disagreement. Separate what happened, how you interpreted it, the response you can attempt, and the result you cannot guarantee. Then ask what responsibilities toward others remain. Compare the reading about roles and duties in section 30 with the opening distinction.

**Check:** give an argument for one recommendation and a serious objection involving injustice, dependence, or a relationship. Explain whether a response can preserve responsibility while revising the interpretation of an impression.

Read Epictetus as a later Greek-speaking Stoic teacher, not as a contemporary of Socrates. The Manual is associated with Arrian's transmission of his teaching.

**School context:** [Diogenes Laertius, Book VII](../sources/Lives-and-Opinions-Yonge.lingua#r_2MZ8K8NBGJSS2844MJ7DDGR849) supplies the wider Stoic accounts of reasoning, knowledge, and nature. Compare a proposed criterion of knowledge with the [sceptical challenge](Sceptics.lingua#r_6KEA1AHWJRDT3ZN8VJQTK06ZFK), then ask how an ethical recommendation depends on claims about agency, value, or the natural order. Keep a later report of a school position distinct from an argument made in the Manual.

**Compare next:** [Epicurus](Epicurus.lingua#r_6KHZKEFRKY1J18Z7TSRAJABZQP) or [the sceptics](Sceptics.lingua#r_6KEA1AHWJRDT3ZN8VJQTK06ZFK). **Return:** [Schools of life](Schools-of-life.lingua#r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
